* Roadmap

- **Why process matters with agents**: agents multiply the work in flight, so
  the team needs one shared record of who does what
- **GitHub project structure**: issue fields, a lean four-status board, the
  Definition of Done, sprints, and carryover
- **Issue workflows**: naming, filing an issue as a spec, labels, updates,
  closing, and splitting work across issues
- **Pull request workflows**: from an issue to a branch and a PR, review
  labels, and small PRs
- **Git branching**: branches as pointers, merge versus rebase, the golden rule
  of rebasing, and team workflows
- **Stacked PRs**: an agent works through a chain of dependent PRs that a human
  reviews and merges in one session
- **Automating the project**: built-in workflows, GitHub Actions, settings kept
  as code, and the issue as the interface to an agent

# Why Process Matters With AI Agents

* Agents Multiply the Work in Flight
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.Stacked_PRs_for_agentic_developent.md:23 '## The Challenge'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.manual_flow.how_to_guide.md:1 '# Goals'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:658 '* From Manual PRs to Agent-Driven PR Creation'

- @Problem@: an agent writes code faster than a human reviews it
  - Several agent sessions run in parallel, each on its own branch
  - Each session ends with a diff that a human must read, test, and merge
  - Human attention, not code generation, becomes the bottleneck

- @Question@: how does the team know which work is in flight, who owns it, and
  what blocks it?

- @Solution@: keep the state of the work in GitHub, not in chat logs
  - An **issue** states the task and its acceptance criteria
  - A **project board** shows the status of every issue
  - A **branch** and a **pull request** carry the change to `master`

- @Key idea@: the conventions written for a human team also let an agent pick
  up, execute, and report a task, with no special-case workflow

* GitHub Objects: Project, Issue, Branch, PR
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:38 '### Projects'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:60 '### Issue'
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.rules.md:67 '## One GitHub Issue Is the Unit of Work'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@:
  - A **GitHub project** groups the issues of one goal
  - An **issue** is one piece of work with one owner
  - A **branch** holds the commits of an issue
  - A **pull request** (PR) asks to merge a branch into `master`

- @Characteristics@:
  - One issue can map to several branches and PRs
  - A PR links back to its issue, so the history stays navigable

- @Key idea@: the issue is the **unit of work**, for a human and for an agent
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

