* A Running Example: One Issue, Three Tasks
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.Stacked_PRs_for_agentic_developent.md:187 '## Implementing Stacked PRs'

// TODO(gp): Check the running example: the issue number and title are
// invented, while the three tasks come from the stacked-PR blog
- @Example@: issue #123 _"Add a user report export"_ in a web app
  - Task 1: add the database schema
  - Task 2: add the API endpoint, which depends on task 1
  - Task 3: add the UI component, which depends on task 2

- @Goal@: follow the issue through the whole lesson
  - Filed on the project board with acceptance criteria
  - Implemented in three branches named after the issue
  - Reviewed as three small PRs
  - Merged bottom-up into `master`

- @Remark@: the tasks form a **dependency chain**
  - Task 2 cannot be reviewed against `master` before task 1 is merged
  - So the three PRs cannot all branch off `master`

# GitHub Project Structure

* GitHub Project: Definition
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.github_projects_process.reference.md:13 '## GH project structure'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.github_projects_process.reference.md:111 '### How to set up a new project'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:38 '### Projects'

- @Definition@: a **GitHub project** is a long-term effort made of issues, which
  several people execute over weekly sprints
  - Unlike an idea, a project has:
    - Clear goals and a defined scope
    - Tangible outcomes
    - A time limit

- @Components@: a project shows its issues through **views**
  - _All issues_: a table of every issue
  - _Current sprint_ and _Next sprint_: the issues of this week and of the next
  - _Backlog_: unfinished issues with no sprint
  - _Team capacity_: the sum of the estimates for each person

- @Characteristics@ of a good project:
  - Created from one organization template, so all projects share views and
    fields
  - Finished in 2-3 months: a longer effort splits into smaller projects
  - Named with a verb phrase, e.g., _"Split cmamp into separate repos"_, not
    _"Config"_

- @Remark@: do not create a new board for each milestone or feature

* Issue Fields
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.github_projects_process.reference.md:37 '### Issue fields'

- @Components@: the fields that every project shares

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Field", "Meaning", "Rule"),
  rows: (
    ("Assignee", "Who owns the issue", "One accountable owner, collaborators named in the issue"),
    ("Status", "Phase of the work", "Required before work starts"),
    ("Label", "Priority and type (e.g., P0, Bug)", "Separate from the status"),
    ("Estimate", "Uninterrupted hours of work", "Best effort, account for each person's time"),
    ("Sprint", "Weekly iteration of the issue", "Planning field"),
    ("Linked PR", "PRs that implement the issue", "Set from the PR description"),
  ),
  caption: "Shared fields of a GitHub project",
  col-widths: (0.8fr, 1.4fr, 2fr),
)
```

- @Remark@: Assignee and Status are required before work starts
  - Sprint and Estimate are planning fields

- @Key idea@: an estimate is a forecast that the team learns from
  - A missed estimate is data for the next plan, not a failure

* A Lean Board With Four Statuses
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.github_projects_process.reference.md:83 '### Lean board with four statuses'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:145 '### Status'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Problem@: a board with 7 statuses (e.g., In Review, PR back to author,
  QA) duplicates the state that the PR already holds

- @Solution@: a small team uses 4 statuses
  - _Todo_: scoped, with acceptance criteria
  - _In Progress_: code, tests, and PR review
  - _Blocked_: a dependency stops the work
  - _Done_: the Definition of Done is met

- @Remark@: an issue is Blocked only if a dependency prevents progress
  - Deferred work goes back to Todo, with a reason
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz
digraph LeanBoard {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.45;
    ranksep=0.40;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.8,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, margin="0.18,0.10",
          height=0.45, fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890",
          fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // normal status : teal | blocked : red

    Todo [label="Todo"];
    Prog [label="In progress"];
    Blk [label="Blocked", fillcolor="#FFB3B3", color="#D64545",
         fontcolor="#6B1F1F"];
    Done [label="Done", penwidth=2.4];

    { rank=same; Prog; Blk; }

    Todo -> Prog [label="  Start"];
    Prog -> Blk [label="  Dependency"];
    Blk -> Prog [label="  Cleared", style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C"];
    Prog -> Done [label="  Definition of Done met"];
    Prog -> Todo [label="  Deferred", style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C",
                  constraint=false];
}
```
label=fig:lean-board
caption=Four statuses of a lean board: teal for normal flow, red for a blocked issue

::::
:::

* Definition of Done
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.scrum.explanation.md:283 '## Definition of Done'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:304 '### Closing an Issue'

- @Definition@: an issue is **Done** when
  - Its acceptance criteria are met
  - The evidence is available
  - The required checks and the review are complete
  - Any required PR is merged

- @Characteristics@:
  - Done means tested, readable, and usable by teammates, not "99% done"
  - Work that is complete for the author is not Done while the review is
    pending
  - Research work without a PR is Done when its result is attached and
    reviewed

- @Remark@: the team can accept 99% as good enough
  - It must be a conscious decision, not a surprise

- @Key idea@: an agent that reports "done" makes a claim, and the Definition of
  Done lists the evidence that the claim needs

* Sprints and Cadence
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:19 '### Sprints'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.github_projects_process.reference.md:201 '## Sprint process'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.github_projects_process.reference.md:250 '### Project-specific cadence'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.github_projects_process.reference.md:264 '### Lean weekly summary'

- @Definition@: a **sprint** is a one-week iteration that answers two questions
  - What should the team focus on this week?
  - What did the team achieve last week?

- @Characteristics@:
  - Default schedule: Sunday to Saturday, with a status update by Friday
  - A project can pick its own start day, and documents it in its docs
    - E.g., the Noesis project runs Thursday to Wednesday sprints
  - A weekly meeting checks the blockers

- @Example@: the lean weekly summary, posted as a project status update
  ```text
  Sprint: <first day> - <last day>
  Goal:
  Completed: issue / merged PR / evidence
  Unfinished: issue / remaining work / carryover decision
  Blockers: issue / cause / unblock owner / next action / follow-up date
  Next sprint: goal / issues / owners
  Weekly meeting decisions:
  ```

* Carryover of Unfinished Issues
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.github_projects_process.reference.md:280 '### Carryover of unfinished issues'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:19 '### Sprints'

- @Problem@: an issue that does not finish in a sprint rolls over silently
  - The board shows the same work "in progress" for weeks
  - Nobody decides whether the work still deserves the time

- @Solution@: treat carryover as a decision
  - Record unfinished work as unfinished, even if most of the code exists
  - Re-plan the carryover explicitly at the weekly meeting
  - Before moving an issue to a later sprint, record the dates, the outcome,
    and the reason in a comment
    - The Sprint field holds one iteration, so it does not keep the history

- @Remark@: urgent work can enter a sprint in the middle of the week
  - Record what was added and what was displaced, so the trade-off is visible

- @Key idea@: an honest board makes overload visible, and overload is the input
  to the next plan

# Issue Workflows

* Naming an Issue
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:186 '### Naming an Issue'
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.rules.md:74 '## Name Branches and PRs After the Issue'

- @Characteristics@ of a good title:
  - Informative, in the form of an action, e.g., _"Do this and that"_
  - Short: details such as the full path of a file go in the body
  - No period at the end
  - Sentence case, not Title Case

- @Example@:
  - **Good**: `Optimize Prometheus configuration for enhanced Kubernetes
    monitoring`
  - **Bad**: `Optimize Prometheus Configuration for Enhanced Kubernetes
    Monitoring`

- @Remark@: the title becomes the branch name
  - Issue 123 _"Add a user report export"_ $\to$ branch
    `HelpersTask123_Add_a_user_report_export`
  - A consistent title gives a consistent history of branches and PRs

* Filing an Issue as a Spec
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:211 '### Filing a New Issue'

- @Definition@: an **actionable issue** describes
  - The problem and why it matters
  - The scope and the explicit exclusions
  - The **acceptance criteria**: the evidence that shows the task is complete
  - The dependencies, the owner, and the effort estimate
  - Pointers to design notes, PRs, and findings

- @Example@: a bug report also includes
  - The command line that was run
  - The error and the stack trace, copy-pasted as text, not as a screenshot
  - The log of the run, possibly with `-v DEBUG`
  - Why the outcome differs from the expected one

- @Remark@: use checkboxes for small steps that do not deserve their own issue

- @Key idea@: an issue written this way is already a spec that an agent can
  execute and that a reviewer can check the PR against

* Labels: Priority and Type Are Not Status
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:112 '### Label'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:120 '#### List of Labels'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.github_projects_process.reference.md:37 '### Issue fields'

- @Definition@: a **label** is an attribute of an issue or of a PR

- @Examples@:
  - _Priority_: `P0` (high), `P1` (important), `P2` (not urgent)
  - _Type_: `Bug`, `Enhancement`, `Documentation`, `Cleanup`, `Design`
  - _Urgency_: `Blocking`, i.e., handle now since it prevents progress
  - _Contribution_: `good first issue`, `Outsource`
  - _PR state_: `PR_for_authors`, `PR_for_reviewers`, `PR_for_integrators`

- @Remark@: priority and status are independent
  - A high-priority issue is not necessarily blocked
  - A blocked issue is not necessarily high priority

- @Key idea@: all repos share one label set, stored in a manifest
  - A query such as _"all `P0` bugs"_ means the same thing in every repo

* Working on an Issue: Updates and Blockers
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:266 '### Updating an Issue'

- @Algorithm@: start an issue
  1. Move it to In Progress
  2. Post a realistic ETA
  3. Post a plan of 3-4 bullets that shows you understood the goal
  4. If the plan is unclear, ask on the issue before you start

- @Characteristics@:
  - At most 2-3 issues In Progress, and finish them before new ones
  - An update at least once a day of work, with pointers to PRs and docs

- @Example@: report a blocker on the issue right away, with a template
  ```text
  Blocker:
  Impact:
  Help needed from:
  Next action and owner:
  Follow-up date:
  ```

* Closing an Issue
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:304 '### Closing an Issue'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:211 '### Filing a New Issue'

- @Definition@: an issue closes when its PR is reviewed and merged into
  `master`
  - The author sets the status to Done, but does not close the issue
  - A team leader or an integrator closes it after the review

- @Characteristics@:
  - Every close has a reason, e.g., _"PR merged"_ or _"Obsolete"_
  - An issue closed as obsolete or duplicate is not reported as delivered
    work
  - Each open checkbox is done, turned into a new issue, or explained
  - Assumptions and loose ends become a `TODO(user)` comment or a follow-up
    issue

- @Key idea@: the issue is the audit trail of a piece of work
  - Its closing comment says what was delivered and what was left out

* Parent Issues, Sub-Issues, and Splitting Work
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.github_projects_process.reference.md:338 '## Parent and sub-issues'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:60 '### Issue'
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.rules.md:67 '## One GitHub Issue Is the Unit of Work'

- @Definition@: GitHub **sub-issues** break a high-level issue (aka an "Epic")
  into smaller issues under a parent

- @Comparison@: two ways to split work that does not fit in a sprint
  - _Sub-issues_: a tree of issues, with the progress tracked on the parent
  - _Independent issues_: each issue is reviewable alone, and references and
    checklists link them

- @Remark@: the convention here is not to use sub-issues
  - Split the work into independently reviewable issues
  - Use one issue with several branches and PRs when the chunks share one goal
    - E.g., the three tasks of issue #123
  - Revisit the convention if hierarchical tracking becomes useful

- @Key idea@: split work by **reviewability**, not by hierarchy
  - Each piece must be checkable on its own

# Pull Request Workflows

* From Issue to Branch
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.rules.md:74 '## Name Branches and PRs After the Issue'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.manual_flow.how_to_guide.md:57 '#### `git_create_issue_and_branch.py`'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.manual_flow.how_to_guide.md:102 '#### `git_branch_create`'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.git.how_to_guide.md:540 '#### Work in a Worktree'

- @Definition@: a branch is named after its issue as
  `<RepoPrefix>Task<IssueNum>_<Description>`
  - A suffix `_<Id>` marks each branch of an issue with several PRs
  - E.g., `HelpersTask123_Add_a_user_report_export_1`

- @Example@: create the issue, the branch, and a worktree in one step
  ```bash
  > git_create_issue_and_branch.py \
      --gh_issue_title "Add a user report export" \
      --suffix 1 --create_worktree
  ```
  - For an existing issue: `invoke git_branch_create --issue-id 123`

- @Remark@: a **worktree** is a second directory of the same repo, checked out
  on its own branch
  - Parallel agent sessions do not share one working copy

- @Key idea@: the name ties every commit, branch, and PR to the issue, so a
  script can find all the work of an issue

* Filing a Pull Request
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:345 '### Filing a New PR'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:367 '#### Filing Process'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.manual_flow.how_to_guide.md:341 '#### `gh_create_pr`'

- @Algorithm@: file a PR
  1. Stage only the files intended for the PR
  2. Give the PR the name of the branch
  3. Write a description that says what to focus on
  4. Link the issue with `Closes #123` if the PR completes it, or with
     `Related to #123` otherwise
  5. Add yourself as assignee, add the reviewers, and set `PR_for_reviewers`
  6. Lint, run the relevant tests, and report the checks you could not run

- @Remark@: GitHub closes the issue when a PR with `Closes #123` merges
  - Use it only in the PR that finishes the issue, so the team controls when
    the issue closes

- @Example@: a draft PR shows a direction or an interface before the work is
  complete
  ```bash
  > invoke gh_create_pr --reviewer user1,user2
  ```
  - `gh_create_pr` opens a draft PR by default, titled after the branch

* Review Labels and Addressing Comments
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:332 '### PR Labels'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:396 '### Review'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:414 '### Addressing a Comment'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: labels track who owes the next action on a PR
  - `PR_for_reviewers`: ready for a review by the team leaders
  - `PR_for_authors`: the author owes changes
  - `PR_for_integrators`: approved, ready for the final review and the merge

- @Algorithm@: address a review comment
  1. Change the code everywhere the comment applies
  2. Mark the comment as resolved, or reply with a reason
  3. Re-request the review and set `PR_for_reviewers`

- @Remark@: the GitHub review state, not a label, is the source of truth
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz
digraph PrLabels {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.40;
    ranksep=0.45;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.8,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, margin="0.18,0.10",
          height=0.45, fillcolor="#FFC98A", color="#D98E2B",
          fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // PR label : orange | merged code : blue

    Rev [label="PR_for_reviewers"];
    Auth [label="PR_for_authors"];
    Int [label="PR_for_integrators"];
    M [label="master", fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0",
       fontcolor="#1F4E79", penwidth=2.4];

    { rank=same; Rev; Auth; }

    Rev -> Auth [label="  Changes requested"];
    Auth -> Rev [label="  Comments addressed", style=dashed,
                 color="#8C8C8C"];
    Rev -> Int [label="  Approved"];
    Int -> M [label="  Merge"];
}
```
label=fig:pr-labels
caption=The labels of a PR (orange) cycle between reviewers and author until the PR merges into master (blue)

::::
:::

* Small PRs Review Faster
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.git.how_to_guide.md:274 '### Pull request (PR) best practices'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.git.how_to_guide.md:264 '#### Keep different changes in separate branches'

- @Problem@: a PR that mixes unrelated changes
  - Is tested and debugged all at once
  - Slows down the review
  - Cannot merge until every change in it is accepted

- @Fact@: review time does not scale linearly with the lines changed
  - Reviewers turn around small PRs faster
// TODO(gp): Check the SmartBear study of code review at Cisco: the 400-line
// figure is recalled from memory and is not in the notes
  - A study of code review at Cisco reports that reviewers find fewer defects
    per line in changes larger than about 400 lines

- @Solution@: frequent, small, self-contained PRs
  - Each PR is coherent and breaks nothing, even if it does not finish the
    issue
  - Early merges give early feedback, e.g., _"you do not need to write any
    code, just do this"_

- @Key idea@: an agent writes a large change in minutes, so the size limit of a
  PR comes from the reviewer, not from the author

# Git Branching

* Branches Are Pointers
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson02.2-Git_Branching.smd:37 '* Git Branching'
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson02.2-Git_Branching.smd:68 '* Git Branching'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.git.how_to_guide.md:231 '#### `master` is sacred'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.git.how_to_guide.md:241 '#### Always work in a branch'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: a **branch** is a movable pointer to a commit
  - `HEAD` points to the current branch
  - A commit moves the pointer of the current branch forward
  - Creating a branch is instantaneous, so branches are cheap

- @Characteristics@ of `master`:
  - Changes only through a PR or a merge
  - Always green: all tests pass and the code is deployable
  - Not a place for development, except for an urgent fix that unblocks
    people

- @Remark@: be the only contributor to your branch when possible
  - Use a branch even for a research notebook, to keep its hacks out of
    `master`
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz
digraph BranchPointers {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.30;
    ranksep=0.35;
    rankdir=LR;

    node [shape=circle, style="filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, width=0.45, fixedsize=true,
          fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // commit : green | branch pointer : orange

    C1; C2; C3; C4; C5;

    Master [label="master", shape=box, style="rounded,filled",
            fixedsize=false, fillcolor="#FFC98A", color="#D98E2B",
            fontcolor="#6B4517", margin="0.12,0.06"];
    Feat [label="HelpersTask123_..._1", shape=box, style="rounded,filled",
          fixedsize=false, fillcolor="#FFC98A", color="#D98E2B",
          fontcolor="#6B4517", margin="0.12,0.06", penwidth=2.4];

    C1 -> C2 -> C3;
    C2 -> C4 -> C5;

    Master -> C3 [style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C", dir=back];
    Feat -> C5 [style=dashed, color="#8C8C8C", dir=back];
    { rank=same; C3; Master; }
    { rank=same; C5; Feat; }
}
```
label=fig:branch-pointers
caption=Commits (green) form a graph, and each branch (orange) is a pointer to its last commit

::::
:::

* Merge Versus Rebase
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson02.2-Git_Branching.smd:277 '* Git Rebasing'
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson02.2-Git_Branching.smd:217 '* Fast Forward Merge'
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson02.2-Git_Branching.smd:370 '* Rebase vs Merge: Philosophical Considerations'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Problem@: `master` and a branch diverge from a common ancestor `C2`

- @Comparison@:
  - _Merge_: `git merge master` adds a merge commit `M` with two parents
    - History records what happened, in parallel
  - _Rebase_: `git rebase master` replays `C4` on top of `master` as `C4'`
    - History looks linear, and replayed commits get new hashes
  - _Fast forward_: with no divergence, Git moves the pointer forward

- @Key idea@: merge and rebase give the same code, but a different history
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```graphviz
digraph MergeRebase {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.10";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.25;
    ranksep=0.25;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=circle, style="filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, width=0.42, fixedsize=true,
          fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.7];

    // commit on master : green | commit of the branch : orange

    subgraph cluster_merge {
        label="After git merge"; labelloc="t";
        fontname="Helvetica-Bold"; fontsize=11; fontcolor="#3A4A5C";
        style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#F7F9FB"; color="#C7D0DA";
        penwidth=1.0; margin=10;
        a1 [label="C1"]; a2 [label="C2"]; a3 [label="C3"];
        a4 [label="C4", fillcolor="#FFC98A", color="#D98E2B",
            fontcolor="#6B4517"];
        am [label="M", fillcolor="#FFC98A", color="#D98E2B",
            fontcolor="#6B4517", penwidth=2.4];
        a1 -> a2 -> a3 -> am;
        a2 -> a4 -> am;
    }

    subgraph cluster_rebase {
        label="After git rebase"; labelloc="t";
        fontname="Helvetica-Bold"; fontsize=11; fontcolor="#3A4A5C";
        style="rounded,filled"; fillcolor="#F7F9FB"; color="#C7D0DA";
        penwidth=1.0; margin=10;
        b1 [label="C1"]; b2 [label="C2"]; b3 [label="C3"];
        b4 [label="C4'", fillcolor="#FFC98A", color="#D98E2B",
            fontcolor="#6B4517", penwidth=2.4];
        b1 -> b2 -> b3 -> b4;
    }
}
```
label=fig:merge-vs-rebase
caption=A merge joins the branch (orange) and master (green) in a commit M, while a rebase replays C4 as C4' on top of master

::::
:::

* The Golden Rule of Rebasing
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson02.2-Git_Branching.smd:344 '* Golden Rule of Rebasing'
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson02.2-Git_Branching.smd:403 '* Rebase vs Merge: Philosophical Considerations'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.git.how_to_guide.md:600 '### Rebasing'
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.execute_with_stacked_prs/SKILL.md:84 '# How the Stack Gets Merged'

- @Problem@: a rebase abandons existing commits and creates new ones that are
  similar but different
  1. You push commits to a remote
  2. Others pull them and build on them
  3. You rewrite the commits with `git rebase` and `git push --force`
  4. Your collaborators must merge their work again

- @Solution@: the **golden rule of rebasing**
  - _Strict_: do not rebase commits that exist outside your repository
  - _Loose_: rebase a branch that only you use, even if it is pushed

- @Characteristics@ of a practical compromise:
  - Use `git pull --rebase` to clean up your local work
  - Use `git merge` on a shared branch
  - Squash-and-merge a branch into `master`, since its commits are only
    checkpoints

- @Key idea@: an agent does not force-push a branch under review without
  telling the user, since the rewrite breaks the review history

* Catching Up With Master: Merge, Rebase, or Squash
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.i_git_merge_master.md:59 '## Three flows for catching up with master'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.i_git_merge_master.md:102 '## Side by side'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.i_git_merge_master.md:137 '## Which one to use'

- @Problem@: a branch has $N$ small commits that touch a region that also
  changed on `master`

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Flow", "Conflicts to resolve", "History", "Rewrites commits"),
  rows: (
    ("Merge", "1", "Non-linear", "No"),
    ("Rebase", "Up to N", "Linear", "Yes, every replayed commit"),
    ("Squash, then merge", "1", "Branch collapses to 1 commit", "Yes, only the branch commits"),
  ),
  caption: "Three flows for the same overlapping changes",
  col-widths: (1fr, 1fr, 1.4fr, 1.4fr),
)
```

- @Intuition@: a merge compares the two sides once, while a rebase replays
  and compares each commit
  - E.g., 5 commits that edit one line stop a rebase 5 times, a merge once

- @Remark@: pick the flow by the state of the branch
  - Shared branch: merge, as `invoke git_merge_master` does
  - Short-lived, unpushed branch: rebase
  - Many "Update" commits: squash, then merge

* Centralized and Forking Workflows
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson02.2-Git_Branching.smd:495 '* Centralized Workflow'
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson02.2-Git_Branching.smd:519 '* Forking Workflows'
// From: data605/lectures_source/Lesson02.2-Git_Branching.smd:552 '* Integration-Manager Workflow'

- @Definition@: a **Git workflow** is an agreed way to collaborate through
  branches and repos

- @Comparison@:
  - _Centralized_: every developer has write access to one shared repo
    - Developers fetch, merge, and push
    - Git rejects a push that is not a fast forward, so nobody overwrites the
      work of others
  - _Forking_ (aka "GitHub workflow"): contributors have read-only access
    - A contributor forks the repo, pushes branches to the fork, and opens a PR
    - A maintainer reviews, accepts, and integrates the PR
  - _Integration manager_: only the maintainer pushes to the official repo
    - E.g., Linux and most open-source projects

- @Remark@: a team inside one organization uses the centralized workflow with
  feature branches and PRs, while open-source projects use forks

- @Key idea@: an agent with write access is a team member in a centralized
  workflow, so branch protection, not trust, keeps it off `master`

# Stacked PRs for Agentic Development

* Long Agent Runs Versus Frequent Review
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.Stacked_PRs_for_agentic_developent.md:23 '## The Challenge'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.Stacked_PRs_for_agentic_developent.md:72 '## Strategy 1: Interactive Workflows'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.Stacked_PRs_for_agentic_developent.md:86 '## Strategy 2: Precompiled Task List'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.Stacked_PRs_for_agentic_developent.md:131 '## Strategy 3: Stacked PRs'

- @Problem@: two goals compete
  - Let the agent run long on a sequence of tasks, ideally a full day
  - Review often, so that the agent does not build on a mistake

- @Comparison@: three strategies for sequential tasks
  - _Interactive_: assign one task, review it, then assign the next
    - Cons: constant context switches, and the human waits for the agent
  - _Precompiled task list_: write all the specs first, then run and review
    each task
    - Cons: many tasks committed on one branch are hard to review, since
      GitHub reviews a PR, not a list of commits
  - _Stacked PRs_: the agent completes all the tasks, one PR per task, and the
    human reviews the stack in one session

- @Intuition@: reviewing five related changes in one block is more productive
  than alternating between dispatch and review

* Stacked PRs: Definition
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.Stacked_PRs_for_agentic_developent.md:131 '## Strategy 3: Stacked PRs'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.Stacked_PRs_for_agentic_developent.md:194 '### GitHub Stacked PRs'

- @Definition@: a **stack** is a chain of dependent PRs
  - The bottom PR targets the trunk (e.g., `master`)
  - Each PR above targets the branch of the PR below
  - GitHub retargets the next PR when the one below merges

```graphviz
digraph Stack {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.10";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.25;
    ranksep=0.45;
    rankdir=RL;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, margin="0.12,0.06",
          height=0.35, fillcolor="#FFC98A", color="#D98E2B",
          fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.2, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.7,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // PR in the stack : orange | trunk : blue

    M [label="master", fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0",
       fontcolor="#1F4E79", penwidth=2.4];
    P1 [label=<<b>PR _1</b><br/>Database schema>];
    P2 [label=<<b>PR _2</b><br/>API endpoint>];
    P3 [label=<<b>PR _3</b><br/>UI component>];

    P1 -> M [label="  Targets"];
    P2 -> P1 [label="  Targets"];
    P3 -> P2 [label="  Targets"];
}
```
label=fig:pr-stack
caption=Issue #123 as a stack: each PR (orange) targets the branch below it, and the bottom PR targets master (blue)

- @Pros@: one uninterrupted work block for the agent and for the human
  - Each PR stays small and reviewable on its own

- @Cons@:
  - _Divergence risk_: a long run can build on a misread spec
  - _Compound complexity_: later PRs depend on earlier ones
  - _Expensive corrections_: a fix low in the stack must reach every PR above

* Building a Stack
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.execute_with_stacked_prs/SKILL.md:6 '# Goal'
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.execute_with_stacked_prs/SKILL.md:54 '## Stack Each Following Branch (`_<ID>`)'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.Use_GitHub_Stacked_PR_workflow.md:62 '## Building a Stack of Branches'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.Use_GitHub_Stacked_PR_workflow.md:183 '## Adopting Existing Branches into a Stack'

- @Algorithm@: the `auto_task.execute_with_stacked_prs` skill
  1. Create the issue and the branch `<BASE>_1` off `master`
  2. Implement the task, run its tests, commit, and push
  3. Create `<BASE>_<ID>` off `<BASE>_<ID-1>`, not off `master`
  4. Open its PR against `<BASE>_<ID-1>`, then go back to step 2
  5. Report the whole stack at the end, with no stop for review

- @Example@: stack task 2 of issue #123
  ```bash
  > invoke git_branch_create --issue-id 123 --suffix 2 \
      --no-only-branch-from-master \
      --no-abort-if-not-master --no-create-pr
  > gh pr create --base <BASE>_1 --head <BASE>_2
  ```

- @Remark@: an untested task compounds into everything stacked on it

- @Remark@: `gh stack init`, `add`, `submit`, and `link` build the same stack
  with the GitHub CLI

* Fixing an Earlier Layer
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.Use_GitHub_Stacked_PR_workflow.md:115 '## Updating an Earlier PR and Cascading the Fix'
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.restack/SKILL.md:6 '# Goal'
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.restack/SKILL.md:70 '## Propagate Changes Down the Stack'
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.restack/SKILL.md:121 '# Constraints'

- @Problem@: a review comment lands on PR `_1`, while PRs `_2` and `_3` build
  on it

- @Comparison@: two ways to propagate the fix up the stack
  - _Rebase cascade_ (`gh stack`): rebase every branch above, then push with a
    lease check
    ```bash
    > gh stack checkout feature/step-1-schema
    > git commit -am "Fix: address review comment"
    > gh stack rebase --upstack
    > gh stack push
    ```
  - _Merge forward_ (`auto_task.restack`): merge each branch into the next one,
    bottom-up, with no rebase and no force-push

- @Characteristics@ of a safe merge forward:
  - Resolve conflicts by the intent of the lower PR, and ask if it is unclear
  - Fetch right before each merge, since another session can push mid-stack

- @Key idea@: the rebase cascade gives a clean history, while merge forward
  preserves the review history at the cost of merge commits

* Merging a Stack Bottom-Up
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.execute_with_stacked_prs/SKILL.md:84 '# How the Stack Gets Merged'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.Use_GitHub_Stacked_PR_workflow.md:150 '## Merging the Stack'
// From: research/ideas/draft.Codebase_automerge.md:26 '## Key Examples'

- @Algorithm@: merge a stack, one PR at a time
  1. Review and merge the bottom PR into `master`
  2. Retarget the next PR to `master`, if GitHub did not do it
  3. Merge `master` into the next branch, resolve the conflicts, and push
  4. Repeat up the stack

- @Remark@: after a squash merge, expect a conflict even on identical content
  - Git cannot tell the squashed commit from the original commits

- @Example@: `gh stack merge <branch> --squash` merges every PR up to that
  branch in one atomic operation
  - Branch protection and required checks apply to every PR in the stack

- @Challenges@ of automating the merge:
  - A conflict during an automatic rebase must stop the stack for a human
  - A green but unapproved PR is rebased, not merged
  - Each merge re-runs CI for every PR above it

- @Key idea@: the agent builds and repairs the stack, while the human decides
  each merge

* When to Stack and When Not To
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.Stacked_PRs_for_agentic_developent.md:175 '## When to Use Each Strategy'
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.execute_with_stacked_prs/SKILL.md:20 '# When to Use This Skill'

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Condition", "Interactive workflow", "Stacked PRs"),
  rows: (
    ("Specs", "Exploratory or uncertain", "Complete and clear"),
    ("Dependencies", "Feedback shapes the next task", "A real dependency chain"),
    ("Cost of divergence", "High", "Low"),
    ("Review style", "Frequent short reviews", "One focused review session"),
    ("Example", "Exploratory tasks", "Refactoring in stages, modular features"),
  ),
  caption: "Choosing an execution strategy for a sequence of tasks",
  col-widths: (1fr, 1.4fr, 1.6fr),
)
```

- @Remark@: confirm the order of the tasks before the run starts
  - A wrong dependency means rebasing the whole stack above it
  - Resolve all the doubts with the user first, so the stack runs without
    interruptions

- @Key idea@: start with interactive workflows, and stack once the task
  sequences are stable and well understood

* Splitting a Large Branch Into PRs
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:684 '* Splitting Work: Child PRs and Branch Splitting'
// From: .claude/skills/github.split_branch_in_PRs/SKILL.md:41 '## Propose PRs'
// From: .claude/skills/github.split_branch_in_PRs/SKILL.md:53 '## Order the PRs'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.manual_flow.how_to_guide.md:537 '### How to Split Large PRs'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/invoke/all.invoke_git_branch_copy.how_to_guide.md:20 '## Introduction'

- @Problem@: a long-lived branch mixes refactoring and behavior changes
  - The reviewer cannot separate _what moved_ from _what changed_

- @Algorithm@: the `github.split_branch_in_PRs` skill
  1. List the files changed against `origin/master`
  2. Group the tightly coupled changes into PRs
  3. Keep whole files in one PR, and each file with its test file
  4. Order the PRs: low risk and many files first
  5. Write `github_PR_plan.md`, plus `pr<NUM>.files.txt` and
     `pr<NUM>.pytest.sh` for each PR

- @Example@: build each child PR from the plan
  ```bash
  claude> /github.create_child_pr PR2
  ```

- @Key idea@: develop a large feature in one shot, but merge it in small chunks
  - `invoke git_branch_copy` copies the branch, and removing code from the copy
    is often easier than adding code to a new branch

# Automating the Project

* Built-in Workflows and GitHub Actions
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.github_projects_process.reference.md:294 '## Automation'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.github_projects_process.reference.md:301 '### Built-in workflows'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.github_projects_process.reference.md:317 '### Github Actions automations'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.use_github.how_to_guide.md:79 '### GH Actions'

- @Definition@:
  - A **built-in workflow** of a project automates a frequent action
    - E.g., when an issue closes, set its status to Done
  - A **GitHub Action** runs a workflow `.github/workflows/<NAME>.yml` on a
    trigger
    - E.g., a push, a PR, or a schedule

- @Example@: `sprint_iteration.yml` moves the items of active projects from
  the last sprint to the current one
  - The Sprint field is an iteration field with a one-week duration

- @Challenges@:
  - Any automation must preserve the Definition of Done
  - A silent rollover of unfinished issues hides the carryover
  - The automation of one organization does not exist in a new project

- @Remark@: update the board by hand, unless a workflow is verified on the
  project
  - Inspect the actual checks: a green PR does not prove that the tests of a
    directory ran if the workflow excludes it

* Configuration as Code: Labels, Projects, Settings
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.synchronize_gh_issue_labels.how_to_guide.md:16 '## Overview'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.sync_gh_projects.how_to_guide.md:33 '## Purpose'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.sync_gh_projects.how_to_guide.md:75 '## Limitations'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.synchronize_gh_settings.how_to_guide.md:17 '## Overview'

- @Problem@: labels, project fields, and repo settings drift apart when each
  repo is configured by hand

- @Solution@: keep each configuration in a manifest, and sync it with a script
  - _Labels_: `sync_gh_issue_labels.py`, from `gh_issues_labels.yml`
  - _Project fields_: `sync_gh_projects.py`, from a template project
  - _Repo settings and branch protection_:
    `dockerized_sync_gh_repo_settings.py`, from a YAML file

- @Characteristics@:
  - A dry run previews the changes before applying them
  - The label and settings syncs back up the current state first
  - The project sync only adds fields, and never deletes a field or a view

- @Challenges@: the GitHub API limits what a script can sync
  - E.g., no API creates a project view, so views are created by hand

* Branch Protection and Code Ownership
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/dev_system/all.synchronize_gh_settings.how_to_guide.md:47 '## Settings manifest file structure'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/git/all.git.how_to_guide.md:231 '#### `master` is sacred'

- @Motivation@: _"`master` is sacred"_ is a convention, while **branch
  protection** is a rule that GitHub enforces on humans and agents alike

- @Example@: the protection of `master` in a settings manifest
  ```yaml
  branch_protection:
    master:
      enforce_admins: true
      allow_force_pushes: false
      required_status_checks:
        contexts: ["run tests"]
      required_pull_request_reviews:
        require_code_owner_reviews: true
        required_approving_review_count: 2
  ```

// TODO(gp): Check the CODEOWNERS definition: it is recalled from the GitHub
// docs and is not in the notes
- @Definition@: a `CODEOWNERS` file maps paths to the people or teams that must
  approve a change to them

- @Key idea@: an agent with write access cannot merge to `master` without
  green checks and a human approval

* The Issue as the Interface to an Agent
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.How_to_run_Claude_on_GitHub.md:28 '# Overview'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.How_to_run_Claude_on_GitHub.md:88 '# Set Up the Repo Workflow'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.How_to_run_Claude_on_GitHub.md:155 '# Common Pitfalls'

- @Example@: `claude-code-action` runs Claude Code inside GitHub Actions
  - Listens for `@claude` in issues, PR comments, and PR reviews
  - Checks out the repo and runs the task
  - Pushes commits and a summary comment back to the PR

- @Characteristics@ of the setup:
  - `.github/workflows/claude.yml` sits on the default branch, since issue and
    comment events read the workflow from there
  - A `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` secret is set on the repo
  - Submodules are checked out if the agent configuration lives in one
  - Assigning an issue does not trigger a run: the text `@claude` must appear

- @Key idea@: each convention of this lesson becomes an input to the agent
  - The issue is the spec
  - The branch name ties the work to the issue
  - The labels and the board report the state
  - Branch protection bounds what the agent can change

* Summary

- @Key idea@: GitHub is the shared state of a team of humans and agents, so its
  conventions matter more as agents produce more work
- A project groups issues toward one goal, and a lean four-status board with a
  strict Definition of Done keeps its state honest
- An issue is the unit of work: named as an action, filed as a spec with
  acceptance criteria, and closed with a reason
- A branch and a PR carry an issue to `master`: named after the issue, small,
  and linked with `Closes` or `Related to`
- Merge is the safe default on a shared branch, and the golden rule of rebasing
  forbids rewriting commits that others use
- Stacked PRs let an agent work through a dependency chain that a human reviews
  and merges bottom-up
- Manifests, sync scripts, and branch protection turn the conventions into
  rules that GitHub enforces

* References

